Acute Otitis Media
Paediatric & Neonatal
Bulging/erythematous tympanic membrane, Otalgia/ear tugging, Fever

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-002
Reason for Visit: Unilateral severe throbbing ear pain with inconsolable crying and high fever requiring urgent evaluation for deep space infection or meningitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated systemic irritability and severe pain unresponsive to basic analgesia. Immediate assessment was required to rule out mastoiditis, central nervous system involvement, and to provide targeted pain relief prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "They have been screaming and pulling at their right ear for hours, and the fever won't break."
History of Present Illness: The patient developed a viral upper respiratory tract infection three days ago, which has now evolved into acute, unilateral, severe throbbing right ear pain. This evening, the pain has escalated, leading to persistent inconsolable crying that notably worsens when the child is placed supine. The parents report frequent tugging and rubbing of the affected ear. There is a new-onset high fever and associated systemic irritability.
Functional Status: Usually independent, currently entirely dependent due to distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver exhaustion from continuous crying over the past six hours.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 125 | Temp: 38.6C | RR: 26 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 14 Kg
General: Flushed, febrile, intermittently crying, easily consolable by caregiver but distressed upon lying flat.
Physical Exam: Otoscopy reveals a bulging, distinctly erythematous right tympanic membrane completely lacking a normal light reflex. There is decreased tympanic membrane mobility on pneumatic testing. The left tympanic membrane is clear. Palpation of the mastoid process yields no tenderness or erythema. Neck is supple with no meningeal signs.
Environmental Observations: Overheated room; recommended removing heavy blankets.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol 15mg/kg given 4 hours ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Otitis Media - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Mastoiditis - Ruled out due to the absence of post-auricular erythema, swelling, and mastoid tenderness.

Otitis Externa - Ruled out due to the absence of tragal tenderness and a clear external auditory canal.

Meningitis - Ruled out due to a supple neck, normal neurological tone, and the child's ability to be consoled when held upright.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of high fever, inconsolable crying, and severe localized otalgia mandated an urgent after-hours physical assessment. Telephone triage recognized the high risk of rapid deterioration, necessitating immediate evaluation to rule out mastoiditis or meningitis. Objective findings confirmed acute otitis media with a bulging, highly erythematous tympanic membrane. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period would have subjected the patient to prolonged, unbearable pain and risked spontaneous tympanic membrane perforation or systemic spread. The immediate intervention provided accurate diagnosis and appropriate systemic analgesia and antimicrobial therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Amoxicillin 15mg/kg PO TDS for 5 days. Advised regular weight-based ibuprofen and paracetamol alternating.
Positive Behavioral Change: Administer analgesia strictly by the clock rather than waiting for the child to cry in pain. Establishing a consistent pain relief schedule reinforces a calm state, breaking the cycle of distress and enabling adequate oral fluid intake.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48-72 hours if symptoms do not improve.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department if the patient develops neck stiffness, photophobia, non-blanching rash, altered conscious state, or visible swelling behind the ear.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic and may evolve despite appropriate therapy.
Clinical Course: Fever and severe pain should begin to subside within 48 hours of initiating antibiotics and regular analgesia. Middle ear fluid may take weeks to completely resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if there is swelling behind the ear, lethargy, or inability to wake the child.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Antibiotics will target the bacterial infection behind the eardrum, while scheduled analgesics will control the severe throbbing pain.
Actionable Steps: Elevate the head of the bed slightly to reduce middle ear pressure. Give fluids frequently in small amounts to encourage swallowing, which helps equalize ear pressure.

